---
import Figure from './Figure.astro';
import { stake } from '../data/site.js';
---
{/*
  THE STAKE, VERSION (a) — NO LONGER ROUTED. Kept whole, and only as the
  other half of an editorial comparison: `src/pages/index.astro` now imports
  `scroll-Stake-alt.astro`, which is this scene with one line changed and its
  phone crop moved off an identifiable face. Everything below still describes
  what both versions do; the reasoning for the swap is in (b)'s own header.
  Delete this file once a human has seen both.

  THE STAKE — the page's one exception, spent once.

  Two facts drove it. The homepage's four best photographs — the Special
  Olympics runner, the Muskingum towers, the mother and infant, the welding
  shop — existed only inside a 224x280px hover plate: measured at eight
  scroll positions they were opacity:0 and naturalWidth:0, so the browser
  never fetched them, and on a phone the plate is display:none. Four
  documentary photographs, referenced in the DOM, rendered to nobody. And
  the 1,059px Pilots ledger that follows this scene holds no image at all.

  So one of them is in the layout now, and it is not in a card. It is the
  scene: full-bleed, 108svh — TALLER THAN THE VIEWPORT, which is the whole
  move. You cannot see the top and the bottom of this photograph at the same
  time. Nothing else on this site is bigger than the frame it is read in.

  It also breaks the column. A left-edge histogram of <main> put 27 text
  elements at exactly x=51; the site's default overture is a micro label at
  that rail, a hairline ruled the full width, a block hung beneath. This
  scene has no label, no rule, no counter and no eyebrow — it is the only
  scene on the site with a single piece of type in it — and that type is set
  RIGHT, into the wall behind the mother, in the negative space the crop was
  chosen to leave. The scrim is mirrored to match (align="right"), so the ink
  is placed where the sentence lands and the left two-thirds of the frame,
  where the subject is, keeps its picture.

  MOTION is the one vector this page had not spent here: the figure enters on
  a bottom-up wipe (the house `enter="wipe"`), and it is the slowest parallax
  on the page, so the photograph is still arriving after the sentence has
  landed. No new vector, no new easing.

  WHY HERE. Method is abstract, Pilots is concrete, and they were consecutive
  and opened identically — same label, same full-width rule, same
  right-aligned counter, one after the other. The photograph is the hinge:
  the mechanism, then the people it is for, then the four pilots by name.

  The crop drops the second adult at the right edge (zoom 1.22 off a 32%
  focal) for one reason: it is where the sentence goes. The corner credit is
  the house treatment and stays the only other mark in the frame.

  THE GROUND, AND A METER THAT WAS LYING ABOUT IT. Wave 4 opened with the
  finding that 79% of this page sits inside a 5.7-point L* band and that this
  scene "dissolves into the same value above and below it". The second half is
  true at the edges. The first half was an instrument artifact:
  `tools/tone-meter.mjs` jumps to a scroll position and samples 420ms later,
  and this figure carries `enter="wipe"` — a 1,180ms bottom-up mask that has
  not finished at 420ms. The meter was photographing the mask, not the
  photograph, and reporting the page ground underneath it. Re-swept with a
  1,500ms settle (same gutters, same 20px step) the page reads:

      navy floor 49% of the scroll, span L* 2.5 → 94.1
      cream       900–1380   L* 93.9
      THIS SCENE 2180–2660   L* 67.3   ← a 64-point step off the ground
      Forum      4200–4420   L* 58.6
      board wall 5640–5800   L* 14.5

  So the ground budget was being spent; the meter could not see it. What was
  real is the dissolve: ~140px of fade at the head of the frame and ~300px at
  the foot, which is what let the scene bleed into the navy at both ends. The
  treatment is opened one stop against that — grade 0.40 → 0.34, vignette 0.50
  → 0.24, and the bottom ramp pulled down and tighter to the sentence's own
  column (rise 0.62 → 0.44, reach 0.46 → 0.44, ink 1.05 → 0.98). Measured:
  the lit run grows 440 → 480px and lifts 62.8 → 67.3 L*. Deliberately small.
  The composition is the wowed moment and the ink still has to carry the
  sentence — `photo-meter` is the constraint, and it stays at 0 failures.

  NOT PINNED. `HeldScene` is spent once site-wide and it is not spent here:
  its beats sit in a band at the foot of the frame, and the whole of this
  scene is one sentence ranged right into the wall at the height the crop
  left empty. Pinning it would rebuild the one composition on this site a
  critic named. See the changelog.
*/}
<section class="stake" aria-labelledby="stake-h">
  <Figure src={stake.image} alt="" ratio="auto"
          grade={0.34} zoom={1.22} focal="30% 42%"
          zoomSm={1} focalSm="50% 36%"
          parallax={3} enter="wipe" loading="lazy"
          scrim="bottom" align="right" reach={0.44} rise={0.44} ink={0.98} vig={0.24}
          credit={stake.credit} creditPos="top-left"
          class="stake__bg" />

  <div class="shell stake__in">
    <h2 class="display display--poster stake__h lines" id="stake-h">{stake.line}</h2>
  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .stake {
    position: relative;
    /* Taller than the screen it is read on. The only element on the site
       that is. 700px is the floor for a short laptop; 1100 the ceiling so a
       tall monitor does not turn it into a second page. */
    min-height: clamp(700px, 108svh, 1100px);
    display: grid;
    align-items: end;
    isolation: isolate;
    overflow: hidden;
  }
  .stake__bg { position: absolute; inset: 0; aspect-ratio: auto; z-index: -1; }

  .stake__in {
    width: 100%;
    padding-block: 0 clamp(2rem, 4.5vw, 3.6rem);
  }

  /* Set RIGHT — the one block on this page that does not begin at the rail.
     The measure is em-fixed so the sentence breaks the same way at every
     width, and the rag is right so the block's hard edge is the frame's
     right margin, opposite the subject. */
  .stake__h {
    margin: 0 0 0 auto;
    /* A NARROW column, not a banner. The measure is set so the four words
       break over three lines and the whole block clears the child's head —
       type laid across a face is the move that makes a photograph read as
       stock. The negative space this crop was chosen to leave is a column,
       so the sentence is set as one. */
    max-width: min(4.5em, 100%);
    text-align: right;
    line-height: 0.98;
    letter-spacing: -0.024em;
    text-wrap: initial;
  }

  @media (max-width: 820px) {
    .stake { min-height: clamp(620px, 104svh, 900px); }
    .stake__in { padding-bottom: clamp(2.6rem, 6vw, 3.4rem); }
    .stake__h { max-width: min(5.2em, 100%); letter-spacing: -0.02em; }
  }
</style>
